export const FINAL_REVIEW = "## Check items\n\n### P — Product goals\n\n- P1: Are the product positioning, target users and core selling points clear;\n- P2: Does the finished video focus attention on the feature priorities confirmed or recorded in the current mode;\n- P3: Are there feature claims that don't exist in the project, are unconfirmed, or are easily misleading.\n- P4: Can the execution choices in the requirement-to-execution decision table be found implemented in the storyboard, copy, page states, audio and assets;\n  a fixed stereotyped \"audience/purpose → style\" mapping must not replace project evidence.\n\n### F — Feature completeness\n\n- F1: Does each \"feature that must be shown\" have at least one clearly recognizable shot;\n- F2: Does each shot provide new information; are there repeated features or repeated taglines;\n- F3: Do page states let the audience understand the feature, rather than being merely decorative motion.\n\n### V — Visual direction\n\n- V1: Do fonts, palette, materials, corner radii, light quality and information density match the confirmed or recorded visual direction;\n- V2: Do motion speed, overshoot, holds and energy curve match the confirmed or recorded motion personality;\n- V3: Does it depart from the production baseline, drifting toward Ink Press, cyber neon or other unrelated styles;\n- V4: Are banned colors, effects or brand characteristics used.\n\n### S — Shot cards and Gallery variants\n\n- S1: Do the shot cards in the feature mapping actually appear in the corresponding shots;\n- S2: When the user named `card name · style name`, do the `style-key`, the exact demo TSX and the Gallery\n  reference sample point to the same variant, and does the finished video use that variant;\n- S3: Compared against the exact demo TSX and the Gallery reference sample, are the recipe card/demo's motion\n  grammar, key timings, easing relationships and mask timing preserved;\n- S4: Are the card's annotated \"Known pitfalls / critical points\" violated;\n- S5: Are the adapted product screenshots, coordinates and brand tokens natural.\n- S6: Have styles marked \"reference only, requires custom implementation\" been explicitly named by the user, with their\n  implementation risk noted in the report; do styles marked \"missing preview\" avoid claiming consistency with a motion sample.\n\nPixel-level consistency with the Gallery samples is not required. What is checked is whether the motion grammar and key parameters are faithful, while\nallowing adjustments of screenshots, coordinates, copy, brand visuals and necessary composition for the target product.\n\n### B — Storyboard consistency\n\n- B1: Do shot order, durations and feature information match the confirmed or recorded final storyboard;\n- B2: Are the main actions, page states, asset sources, captions, transitions and SFX consistent;\n- B3: Is the brand wordmark hold at least 1 second, and does batch motion end with at least 0.5 seconds of stillness;\n- B4: After the final storyboard was released, were key shots deleted, replaced or added without justification.\n\n### D — Data and asset safety\n\n- D1: Is the real data, fictional data or anonymization policy followed;\n- D2: Is any customer, personal, secret, internal address or other sensitive information exposed;\n- D3: When showing real product pages, are real screenshots used rather than low-quality hand-built reproductions;\n- D4: Are screenshot states, fonts, images and dynamic data fully loaded.\n- D5: Does public demo data have a verifiable public nature; have customer, personal, internal, secret, live\n  or other sensitive data been made fictional, anonymized or frozen.\n\n### A — Audio and rhythm\n\n- A1: Does the BGM match the confirmed or recorded audio direction, and does its volume leave room for SFX and optional voiceover;\n- A2: Are SFX pinned to real action keyframes; is there obvious misalignment;\n- A3: For strong-rhythm music, is the cut point error no more than 3 frames;\n- A4: Do the ending energy peak and the riser→impact→sparkle phrase hold up (if the storyboard uses them; the sparkle beat is taken from `sfx/light/`);\n- A5: Have all long samples (>5s, 21 in the library) been given an explicit `durationInFrames`; does any sound effect keep sounding after the action ends;\n- A6: Is every SFX actually audible in the **rendered output** (don't rely on the preview — the preview clamps volume to 1.0); for assets that are inherently recorded quietly (peak <-12dB, 7 in the library), which may still be buried by the BGM even at 1.0, have they been swapped / pre-normalized / given sufficient >1 gain, with max_volume not clipping after rendering;\n- A7: Are UI-type sound effects real object textures rather than synthesized feedback prompt sounds (the S1 deciding question) — if synthesized sounds in `sfx/ui/` such as `ui-confirm-*` `ui-*-tone` `ui-notify-*` appear in the finished video, is there a narrative reason of \"the system is speaking\" and has it been written into the project documentation (per-file texture table in sound-design 3.3).\n- A8: For videos with BGM, have both the with-BGM / without-BGM (keeping SFX) versions been delivered,\n  and do both come from the same timeline (the without-BGM version's picture and SFX are frame-by-frame identical to the with-BGM version);\n- A9: For beat-synced/SFX-dense videos, have the output audio track offset and source peak\n  lag been measured on the finished video and compensated (sound-design §4.6); does the compensation constant come with a measurement record (the four render pipeline elements,\n  probe, measurement method, date); are source analysis values and the output offset accounted for separately (SOURCE_BEAT0\n  not contaminated); is the post-render back-test error table free of systematic same-direction offset (music-beat-sync §5b).\n\n### Q — Visual technical quality\n\nExecute `aesthetic-rules.md` in full. At minimum check text sharpness, composition, rounded-corner masks, 3D texture\nclarity, element placement, camera shake, transition seams, cheapness of light effects, readable duration and deterministic rendering.\n\n## Report format\n\nEach conclusion uses an ID, and issues come with frame numbers or screenshots:\n\n```text\nP1 ✓ Product positioning and core selling points are clear.\nF2 ✗ Frame 340: the confirmed knowledge graph feature does not appear in the finished video.\nV3 ✗ Frame 420: large areas of amber paper material depart from the confirmed deep-blue tech direction.\nS2 ✗ Frame 515: the user chose whip-pan, but it was actually implemented as flash-cut.\nQ2 ✗ Frame 615: body text is blurry after the 3D push-in; see key frame f615.png.\n```\n\nFinally, list must-fix items, suggested improvements and unverifiable items by severity. Do not replace item-by-item checks with an unsupported\n\"overall good\", and do not mark everything as passed to make the report look good.";
/** aesthetic-rules.md: every rule's ID, heading and rule line. */
export const AESTHETIC_RULES = "R1. Key information must breathe after it lands; the brand wordmark holds for a full 1 second\n  elements carrying information (title cards / numbers / the full view of a grid) stay still for ≥1s after landing before cutting; breathing pauses are given preferentially to brand memory points (the moment the wordmark lands), not to ordinary content cards.\nR2. A sense of speed comes from acceleration, not uniform fast motion; attach group motion to a physical metaphor, and leave a 0.5s pause at the end\n  fly-ins/scrolls always use non-linear easing + non-uniform staggering; uniform motion reads as a cheap PPT; entrances of many similar elements use a hard-accelerating \"faster and faster\" beat, ideally bound to a physical metaphor (e.g. dealing cards from a deck), and once the board is full, stay still for 0.5s before cutting to the next beat.\nR3. Rhythm: rather slow than fast — slow the first version down a notch by default, main action arc ≥3s, interaction demos at real human operating speed\n  the opening subject's action (focus → float → return, one complete arc) takes at least 3 seconds; simulated real interactions (typing, filtering, clicking) go at the speed a real person would operate, so the audience can follow along; when laying out the timeline, reserve hold/rest frame budgets — the first version is almost always too fast.\nR4. Beat sync constrains timing, not amplitude: whole-frame beat impacts ≤3 in the whole video; by default only move the element layer\n  in strong-rhythm BGM videos, \"cut points on the beat\" does not equal \"hitting the picture on every beat\" — impacts acting on the whole frame/camera layer (whole-frame scale pumps / screen shake / flash frames / negative frames) are managed as big slams: ≤3 in the whole video, pinned to the strongest-hit list of music-beat-sync §2b, with ≥16 beats between any two adjacent ones; all other on-beat motion acts only on the hero element layer; beat-pump-type consecutive hits ≤4 beats per instance and ≤1 instance in the whole video. The kick hit table is a candidate pool, not a trigger. The counting unit is one use of a technique/set-piece, not the number of internal sub-events: one application of a card (paparazzi triple flash, trailer-bumper triple cut) counts as one slot; the burst structure and whole-video count limits noted on the card itself still apply, and are not counted again frame by frame because of this rule.\nQ1. Reproducing existing pages must use real screenshots; hand-built UI is limited to non-reproduction scenarios and must meet quality/expression standards; data is handled by its risk policy\n  for any shot showing an existing page of the product, the first step is to use a headless browser to capture the real page (full-page texture + element-level cutouts + coordinate json); do not hand-build simulated textures to reproduce it; non-reproduction scenarios (abstract openings / brand segments / standalone showcase components that don't exist on the page) allow hand-built UI, but quality (publication-grade texture, against the Q10 standard) and clarity of expression (the audience understands at a glance what it is) are hard thresholds; if they aren't met, go back to the screenshot route. Public demo data may be kept only after the product brief explicitly confirms it; customer, personal, internal, secret, live or other sensitive content must first be made fictional, anonymized or frozen. The same policy applies to hand-built assets.\nQ2. Under 3D perspective, rasterize UI textures at native size and then use them scaled down; when text is blurry, check the texture resolution chain first\n  UI textures in 3D scenes are natively rasterized at 2–4 times the display size and downsampled in 3D (magnification goes through layout-level CSS zoom rather than transform scale); when text is blurry, check the rasterization path first; don't touch camera/DoF first.\nQ3. Shots must be steady: product promos do not add handheld shake by default\n  handheld shake is banned in bright-scene UI promos; camera noise is used, at a very small value, only in dark-scene atmospheric videos explicitly pursuing a documentary feel, and you must self-check rendered frames to confirm there is no unintended shake.\nQ4. Highlight/sweep effects: better none than bad — no mass application, at most once per shot for the hero, and must be clipped within the rounded-corner boundary\n  glint/bloom-sweep type effects are not applied en masse — batches of elements enter through the motion itself rather than by glowing one by one; retained light effects must be clipped by the carrying element's border-radius/overflow; light spilling over rounded corners is a typical source of cheapness. The light-effect technical path itself is not banned: a single sheen/bloom, given to the hero, with solid implementation quality, can be done; after doing it, ask yourself against the rendered frames \"does it look good?\" before handing it in.\nQ5. The opening has only one hero: a single subject + a complete action arc beats a group dance\n  opening information should be sparse and focused — one hero element, one complete arc (spotlight → push in → float → outline light → return); multiple elements dancing together cannot carry a first impression.\nQ6. Camera angle serves readability: information-dense shots head-on, text close-ups level from the side, stylized tilts verified shot by shot\n  stylized camera angles are decided shot by shot — narrative/emotional shots may tilt, information-dense shots like lists/stacks stay head-on; close-ups of cards containing text use a level side angle, avoiding steep low/high angles that destroy text readability; applying one camera style globally across the board is forbidden.\nQ7. The four-piece object close-up: side tilt angle + perceptible height + orbit + contrasting dark material background\n  object/asset close-up shots give full play to the environment — a side tilted camera angle, stacking up to a perceptible volume/height, the camera orbiting the subject, a background of dark material contrasting with the main scene (e.g. brushed metal), then a synchronized pull-back to transition back to the main scene.\nQ8. Make the ending a \"launch event group photo\": elements from the whole video fly in from all directions to surround the wordmark, pushing energy to the video's peak\n  the close of a multi-feature product uses a \"family photo\" structure — pick one representative element from each feature already shown to fly in from multiple directions and freeze; its scale matches the whole video's energy peak (crane camera move + stage lighting + particles); first-version endings are almost always too conservative, so step it up a notch by default.\nQ9. The end point of a fly-in animation must be a real slot in the page layout; elements do not float above the page\n  after flying in, elements must \"embed\" into the page (return to a real grid slot, extend the scroll flow); floating above it without landing looks fake.\nQ10. Mock content in document-type shots must be publication-grade: native typography, text filling the space, the complete layout in frame\n  the audience will read every word on screen — mocks for document/report-type shots must use the product's native typography filled with fake content at real density, with layout elements like the sidebar/comment column fully in frame; a \"sticker + slogan\"-level fake document means reworking the whole shot.\nQ11. Text meant to be read has a minimum effective glyph height: captions ≥5% of frame height, auxiliary text ≥3%; measured in final pixels, not fontSize\n  1080p finished videos calibrate readability for phone/embedded small-window playback — shot narrative captions effective glyph height ≥56px (≥5.2% of frame height, 60px tier recommended), auxiliary text such as subtitles/statistics/URLs ≥32px (≥3%); effective glyph height = fontSize × all ancestor scales × perspective compression (for 3D shots estimate with `cos(rotY)`); acceptance measures actual pixels on rendered frames, not the fontSize in code. Text has only two states: \"texture\" (decorative small text in screenshot cutouts, which must be clearly blurred/dimmed so the audience doesn't try to read it) or \"to be read\" (meets the size threshold + sufficient contrast, with a scrim/drop shadow when over a light background); there is no in-between state of \"re-laid out but still unreadable\" — text that can't be read is better deleted. The closing URL/call to action is the one line in the whole video that should least be small.\nS1. Choose music and sound effects by \"genre\", not by \"event\": strong-drum electronic bed + cinematic SFX vocabulary; game sound pack timbres banned (ban the timbre, not the action)\n  product promo SFX vocabulary = whoosh (camera move) / impact (landing) / riser (build-up) / sparkle (light effect) / transition (transition); game sound pack timbres are banned; for BGM choose a strong-drum, strong-rhythm electronic bed (tech-house type); candidate tracks must be laid under the finished video for listening — you cannot judge the temperament by listening to the track alone.\nS2. Manage SFX pinned to frames beat by beat; for rapid-fire sound effects use two-sample alternation + stepped volume decrease + accelerating intervals, not repetition at the same loudness\n  SFX are centrally managed in a declarative table of \"frame number + source + volume\", each entry commented with its corresponding picture action, eliminating sound effects scattered by feel; when similar elements fire in succession, prevent the machine-gun effect with three tricks — two-sample alternation, stepping volume down along the sequence (e.g. 0.40→0.25 in six steps), intervals accelerating to follow the animation curve, and when it gets so dense it blurs together, let the sound fade out into a swoosh (this repo has empirically no playbackRate pitch shifting; anti-machine-gun doesn't rely on pitch shifting).\nS3. Do sound after the picture is locked; once the timeline moves, re-pin the whole SFX table\n  BGM/SFX are laid down after the picture structure is basically locked; for any change that alters shot duration/order, the wrap-up action always includes \"realign SFX frame numbers across the whole table\" — audio-picture misalignment is a must-check item before delivery.\nS4. Foley takes priority over decorative sound: picture actions get the real sound of that action, and the audio is trimmed to exactly the length of the action\n  whatever action happens in the picture gets the corresponding sound (typing visuals get keyboard sounds, items dropping in one by one get individual pops); a generic swoosh can't cover distinctive picture actions; long samples are trimmed by picture segment (Sequence truncation), with duration exactly equal to the action.\nS5. Audio-picture alignment adds two offsets: measure the output audio track offset per render pipeline and subtract it uniformly; measure the source peak lag per file\n  the entire audio track of the rendered finished video has a fixed lag relative to the video; the root cause is in the output encoding/muxing chain (AAC encoder priming, typically 2048 samples at 48kHz ≈1.28f@30fps; tracked by Remotion in remotion-dev/remotion#7099), not in the behavior of `<Audio>`/`Sequence.from` — measure and record it per render pipeline (Remotion version + codec + sample rate + container) (extract the audio track from the rendered video and do normalized cross-correlation with the original files of 2–3 sharp SFX; it counts as measured accurately only if the residuals are consistent); subtract the compensation uniformly in the render layer's from formula, don't change frame numbers entry by entry; the source's own peak lag (peak not at the file head) is measured per file. Unified formula `from = target peak frame − peak lag − output offset`; for the method and boundaries (truncated-type sound effect windows are not lengthened; within the first 2f of the video it can't be fully compensated) see sound-design §4.6.\nC1. After the picture is locked, rewrite the copy once against the final shots; pure animation segments also get narration\n  draft copy is only a placeholder — after the picture is finalized, rewrite shot by shot against the finished video; pure animation shots get short explanatory captions, leaving no \"mute segments\" (except the brand closing shot, which is kept clean).\nC2. Taglines are written as \"team + product feature name + concrete benefit\"; abstract metaphor words are always made concrete\n  taglines should be concrete and include product feature names (Paper Radar / weekly report), avoiding abstract metaphor words (generic slogans of the board/place kind); add a lead-in title card before important features appear.\nC3. Annotation text in 3D shots lives in the same 3D space: page-space, same perspective, angled shot, large type + highlighter highlight\n  when adding in-frame annotations to a subject in a 3D scene, the text must enter the same 3D space (page coordinate system, the same camera perspective, translateZ float + cast shadow); flat overlaid text is disconnected from the scene; styling uses large serif type + marker highlight on keywords.\nP1. Before delivery you must render, take frames and check them one by one yourself; don't hand the first review to the user\n  after each round of changes, first render key frame screenshots yourself and check them one by one (composition / shake / text sharpness / continuity errors); if necessary, prove it with pixel-level tools (crop-and-zoom / two-frame diff), then hand it to the user.\nP2. First break reference material down frame by frame into a technique list, then judge suitability shot by shot; do not apply it globally\n  if there is a reference video, first extract frames and do a motion breakdown (a list of how each animation/transition/effect is implemented) and write it into the spec, then keep or drop by project suitability; image references describe the feel of \"a certain kind of shot\", not a style order for the whole video — before implementing, judge shot by shot whether it applies, and self-check after changes before handing in.\nP3. For feedback with ambiguous references, confirm the target first; if you change the wrong thing, revert the whole commit cleanly; don't patch on top of the wrong version\n  for vague feedback like \"the first title\", confirm what's meant before acting; if the wrong target was changed, immediately revert the whole commit and redo it; keep each change as an independent commit so it can be rolled back cleanly.\nP4. First list the feature inventory and map each to a shot; one animation technique is the star only once in the whole video; repeated shots/information are always deleted\n  before shooting, list the product feature inventory (dashboard / details / papers / weekly report / brand) and map each to a shot; missing a core feature means rework; one technique (fly-in/stacking/page-turn) is the star only once in the whole video; editing self-check filters by \"what new information does this shot give\"; the same asset/information appears only once in the whole video; repetition means deletion.";
